WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.500
ABI means Application Binary Interface: the binary-level contract compiled code depends on when one component calls another.

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:17.960
It is the agreement about how functions are called, how objects are laid out, how symbols are named, which runtime behavior is expected, and how resources cross a boundary.

3
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.940
Source code compatibility is not the same thing.

4
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.400
A header may still compile while the binary contract expected by an existing caller has changed.

5
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:35.960
ABI instability can produce crashes, corrupted state, mismatched cleanup, plugin load failures, data layout confusion, and unsafe upgrade paths.

6
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:42.060
These failures are often difficult to diagnose because the source code may look correct on both sides of the boundary.

7
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.400
A caller built against one view of a type may run against another.

8
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.620
A plugin may assume a different allocation rule.

9
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.440
A library may expose a symbol that downstream code was never supposed to depend on.

10
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:05.280
Defensive ABI design matters whenever C++ libraries, shared objects, static libraries, SDKs, plugins, or third-party extensions interact.

11
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:07.620
It is not only a packaging concern.

12
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:19.000
ABI stability supports reliable updates, controlled compatibility promises, predictable cleanup, and safer integration with modules built by different teams or vendors.

13
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:27.260
The practical starting point is to identify binary boundaries deliberately and treat them as security-relevant engineering contracts.

14
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.760
If the boundary is part of a product promise, it deserves the same release discipline as source code and configuration.

15
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.560
In practice, that discipline starts before release week.

16
00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:44.940
Teams should decide which interfaces are binary promises, who owns them, how changes are reviewed, and what evidence is needed before shipping.

17
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:55.200
A stable ABI gives operations teams safer upgrade paths and gives developers a clearer line between supported extension points and private implementation details.
